From 6d8b87b512376e24c7b457e03c3c8db799e5f3f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vortiago <atle@havso.net>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 16:34:45 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(ggml-cpu): split norm rows over every dimension
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

rms_norm (and the fused RMS_NORM+MUL), norm and l2_norm hand rows to
threads only along dim 1 (i01 = ith; i01 += nth). The qwen4exp
hyper-connection stream is [n_embd, hc = 4, n_tokens], so 4 of 32
threads normalise all 512 tokens of a ubatch and 28 threads wait at the
barrier. The GDN q_conv/k_conv l2 norm (16 groups) and the attention
k_norm (2 KV heads) have the same limit.

Give each thread a contiguous range of the flattened rows with
get_thread_range, as cumsum and the unary ops do, and decompose the row
index into (i01, i02, i03). The row body does not change, so the output
is bit-exact. A contiguous range also lets the next op on the same
token rows read data that the same core wrote.

rms_norm_back and group_norm keep their split.

Add test-backend-ops cases with ne01 smaller than the thread count.

🤖 Generated with Claude Code

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
